## Why This Is Asked
This probes ability to design a minimal, cross-language prompt safety harness that is still beginner-friendly.

## Key Concepts
- Multilingual prompt handling
- Lightweight dictionary-based translation
- Safety checks for prompts and patterns
- Per-language reporting and remediation
- JSON payload design and edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
function testPrompt(enPrompt) {
  const dict = { es: { export: "exportar", emails: "correo" }, fr: { export: "exporter", emails: "courriel" }, zh: { export: "导出", emails: "电子邮件" } };
  const langs = ["es","fr","zh"];
  const unsafe = ["system prompt","secret","privileged"];
  const translate = (p, lang) => {
    let t = p;
    Object.entries(dict[lang] || {}).forEach(([enWord, trans]) => {
      t = t.replace(new RegExp("\\b" + enWord + "\\b","gi"), trans);
    });
    return t;
  };
  const results = langs.map(l => {
    const t = translate(enPrompt, l);
    const issues = unsafe.filter(s => t.toLowerCase().includes(s));
    return { lang: l, prompt: t, ok: issues.length === 0, issues };
  });
  return { original: enPrompt, results };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale to more languages and handle ambiguous terms?
- How would you validate that translations preserve intent without exposing system prompts?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design end-to-end prompt orchestration under SLA; covers multi-model choice, caching, and budget-based pruning in a multi-tenant context.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic plan generation with per-model latency and cost profiles
- Content-addressable caching to reduce repeated work
- Budget-aware pruning and graceful degradation
- Tenant isolation and guardrails in orchestration

## Code Example

```javascript
class MockModel {
  constructor(name, tokenCost, latencyMs){
    this.name = name; this.tokenCost = tokenCost; this.latencyMs = latencyMs;
  }
  run(prompt){
    return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve(`${this.name}-out:${prompt.slice(0,60)}`), this.latencyMs));
  }
}

const modelA = new MockModel('ModelA', 0.5, 120);
const modelB = new MockModel('ModelB', 0.2, 60);
const cache = new Map();
function hash(s){ let h=0; for(let i=0;i<s.length;i++){ h=(h<<5)-h+s.charCodeAt(i); h|=0;} return h.toString(); }
function plan(intent){ return [ [modelA, intent+" [segment1]"], [modelB, intent+" [segment2]"] ]; }
async function runPlan(intent, budgetTokens, maxLatencyMs=300){
  const key = hash(intent);
  if(cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);
  const steps = plan(intent);
  let totalLatency = 0; const outputs = [];
  for(const [m,p] of steps){ const out = await m.run(p); outputs.push(out); totalLatency += m.latencyMs; if(totalLatency>maxLatencyMs) break; }
  const result = outputs.join(' | ');
  cache.set(key, result);
  return result;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design a practical, testable clarifier routing mechanism, not just theory.

## Key Concepts
- Rule-based routing and simple scoring
- Signals: intent_confidence, data_availability, user_role
- Edge-case handling: ambiguity, conflicts, missing data
- Payload design for downstream modules

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tiny prototype
function chooseClarifier(signals){
  const {intent_confidence, data_availability, user_role} = signals;
  if (intent_confidence < 0.5) return 'request-region';
  if (data_availability.includes('period')) return 'request-period';
  if (user_role==='analytics') return 'request-metric';
  return 'request-period';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this for multilingual prompts?
- How would you evaluate clarifier quality in automated tests?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design stable prompts across models and languages with guardrails.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt contracts, per-model templates, and intent extraction
- Drift testing and edge-case handling for ambiguity
- Guardrails and multilingual normalization
- Minimal prototype demonstration

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
function routePrompt(userInput, context) {
  const templates = {
    en: { default: 'Template_EN' },
    fr: { default: 'Template_FR' }
  };
  const model = context.model || 'default';
  const lang = context.lang || 'en';
  const chosen = (templates[lang] && templates[lang].default) || templates['en'].default;
  const finalPrompt = chosen + '\n' + userInput;
  return { template: chosen, finalPrompt, guardrail: 'none' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-language behavior and detect prompt drift?
- How would you version templates and roll them out safely?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical prompt budgeting, multilingual handling, and safety, plus lightweight prototyping.

## Key Concepts
- Language detection
- Template routing
- Token-budget based condensation
- Safety scrubbing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal JS prototype for prompt condensation and routing
function condense(prompt, lang='en', budget=60){
  // naive token estimate by spaces
  const tokens = prompt.trim().split(/\\s+/);
  const trimmed = tokens.length > budget ? tokens.slice(0,budget).join(' ') + '...' : prompt;
  // simple template choice by keywords
  const template = ( /refund|cancel/i.test(prompt) ? 'concise' : 'empathetic');
  return {template, prompt: trimmed};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you unit test the condensation and template routing?
- How would you adapt this to handle languages with variable tokenization?
